HST Office: a GNOME/Cinnamon log out keeps the open documents and their changes.

The gtk3 plugin follows the GNOME session protocol (org.gnome.SessionManager). On EndSession (and
Stop) it cleared the modified flag of every document and terminated: AutoRecovery then took the
documents for unchanged and deleted their recovery copies, the unsaved-documents store kept nothing,
and the next start showed nothing. Measured on 1.0.1 and rc/1.0.2-batch5 with a session manager on a
private bus: a document changed before and after its last recovery copy was lost completely, without a
question.
- vcl gtkframe.cxx: before the flags are cleared (and before EndSession is answered), AutoRecovery
  stores the session synchronously, as the X11 session management of the gen plugin does
  (SessionListener): doSessionSave writes every document to its recovery file, doSessionQuietQuit
  closes the documents keeping their entries (modified state included) and marks the session
  (RecoveryInfo/SessionData). The next start restores the session without a dialog, the documents
  carry their changes and are still marked modified. Every frame gets the signal: the first one
  stores and ends the office; one arriving while the documents are stored (the main loop runs) only
  answers, a nested terminate left the office running. Without AutoRecovery (--norestore) nothing
  changes.
License: these upstream files remain under their original license (MPL-2.0).
Test: tests/runtime/check-session-end-gnome.py (with tests/runtime/fake_gnome_session.py).
